    "available_at": "2026-10-19T16:50:01.597786+00:00",
    "body": "The new policy signals an escalation by the Chinese government on biopharma deals but is unlikely to have a major impact given that the vast majority of foreign interest in Chinese biotech comes in the form of licensing agreements.\nChina’s government has proposed a ban on the sale of certain biotechnology platforms, including cell and gene therapy tech—but the policy does not apply to the lucrative licensing deals that have been proliferating in the region.\nThe new policy from China’s ministries of Commerce and Science and Technology bans the transfer of ownership for five technologies related to cell and gene therapy, small nucleic acid drugs and pathogenic microorganisms. An exemption allows for licensing transactions in these technologies, however. The new policy was released on Friday and will be open to public comment.\nWhile the policy is an escalation of the Chinese government’s oversight of intellectual property, it’s unlikely to have much impact over the ongoing partnerships that have defined the biotech industry over the past couple years.\nLicensing deals for China-based assets have grown to 58% of all licensing deal value so far this year, according to a recent analysis from global investment bank Locust Walk. This compares to 26% for U.S.-based companies.\nCHINA\nWebinar: The China question: Can US biotechs lead as investment heads east?\nIn this webinar, BioSpace will gathered experts to discuss China’s impact on the future of biotech and how both U.S. and Chinese companies can thrive—and maybe even collaborate.\nOctober 15, 2026\n·\n1 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more\nHowever, full acquisitions of Chinese technology by foreign firms are rare. So far this year, U.S. companies have accounted for 91% of all M&A deal value, whereas China-based biotechs made up just 1%, according to Locust Walk. Of the 89 acquisitions so far this year, just 6% were for China-headquartered assets or companies compared to 78% for U.S. firms. That was, however, still twice as many as last year, when 3% of 80 M&A deals involved products or companies from China.\nIn September, the Chinese government released a five-year plan for the biotech industry, seeking to transform the rising sector into a $522 billion powerhouse by 2030. The plan lays out a path to having Chinese-developed assets achieve a quarter of all global blockbuster drugs on the market.\nThe dramatic policy did not surprise industry insiders after previous plans to bolster the sector have delivered as promised.\n“They are executing,” said Jeremy Levin, executive chair of Ovid Therapeutics, during a BioSpace webinar last week. “They’re faster. They’re better. They are doing what they said they would.”\nPatroski Lawson, founder of KPM Group and the American Biotech Innovation Alliance, said the U.S. needs to similarly develop a plan by bringing together all stakeholders. He also called for a movement to reach everyday Americans to stress the importance of a homegrown industry.\n“China is gaining on us,” Lawson said. “What are we going to do about it? Are we going to continue complaining? Are we going to get our act together?”\nChina\nChina plans to transform biotech sector into $522B powerhouse by 2030\nA new five-year biotech plan has been released by the Chinese government, revealing ambitious goals such as achieving five blockbuster medicines on the global markets in that time.\nSeptember 18, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more",
